{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_005 inherits the verification discipline of program:hv_floor by exhaustively grounding minimality: it checks all three prior candidates {3,8,13} against the second congruence and closes the range [1,17], which is exactly the floor-complete sweep that program:hv_floor demands before an answer is admitted. This relation is REFUTED IF the enumeration of x≡3 mod 5 below 18 is incomplete or if hv_floor-style verification were already subsumed by a pure algebraic derivation making the empirical sweep redundant for proof. The dependence is on SRC_005.","neighbours":["SRC_005","SRC_004","SRC_002","SRC_001"],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"SRC_004 integrates SRC_002's general CRT residue-class result n≡18 mod 35 with SRC_005's exhaustive minimality check: the algebraic derivation (5a+3≡4 mod 7, a≡3 mod 7, x=18) supplies the candidate, and the exhaustive sweep below 18 supplies the floor-complete guarantee that no smaller member of the residue class was missed. This relation is REFUTED IF either the algebraic method alone is self-certifying for minimality (rendering the sweep decorative) or if the two derivations disagree on the answer. The dependence is on SRC_004.","neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_002","SRC_005","SRC_003"],"typicality":0.82},{"content":"SRC_001's '-2' unified-remainder shortcut abstracts to the general constant-difference CRT identity (when r1-d1 = r2-d2, then x≡d-1 mod lcm(d1,d2)), which SRC_003 partially applies and then abandons because the actual differences are -2 and -3. This relation is REFUTED IF the constant-difference shortcut, when correctly restricted to equal differences, yields a residue class not already capturable by the standard CRT parametric method, i.e., if it is a genuinely independent solution path rather than a strict specialization. The dependence is on SRC_001.","neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_003","SRC_002","SRC_004"],"typicality":0.58},{"content":"SRC_003's reasoning contradicts itself internally: it first claims a '-2' common residue that would give x≡33 mod 35, then discovers 33 fails mod 7, then silently switches to an 'a≡5 mod 7' computation that gives x=28 before further correcting to x=18 — making it the only neighbourhood artifact that is REFUTED by its own internal verification. This relation is REFUTED IF SRC_003's final stated answer (x=18) is taken as the operative claim independent of the failed derivations preceding it, or if the mid-derivation value 28 actually satisfies both congruences (28 mod 7=0≠4, so it does not). The dependence is on SRC_003.","neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_001","SRC_002","SRC_005"],"typicality":0.42},{"content":"SRC_002 and SRC_005 share the mechanism of parametric-enumeration: both generate candidates by substituting x=5a+3 into the second congruence (SRC_002 via the modular inverse to get a≡3 mod 7 and then x=18; SRC_005 by a full sweep of a=0,1,2 to find the match at a=3). They are two instantiations of the same parametric bridge between the two congruence systems. This relation is REFUTED IF SRC_002's inverse-based derivation and SRC_005's exhaustive sweep rely on mutually incompatible algebraic structures or if either does not use the x=5a+3 parametric form. The dependence is on SRC_002.","neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_005","SRC_004","SRC_003"],"typicality":0.72},{"content":"SRC_003 abstracts SRC_005's CRT solution by identifying that 4≡-3 mod 7 and 3≡-2 mod 5 share the structural form of residues expressible as (divisor - offset), but the offsets differ (-2 vs -3), so the unified constant-difference identity fails while the general CRT parametric solution succeeds. This relation is REFUTED IF the constant-difference shortcut is actually a correct application here (which would mean 18 is wrong and 33 is right — but 33 mod 7=5≠4 refutes that). The dependence is on SRC_003.","neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_001","SRC_005","SRC_002"],"typicality":0.50}]}